<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dutch Golden Age</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Golden Age was the period, roughly the seventeenth century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> was among the richest and most powerful states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> dominated world trade and finance, the East and West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Companies built an overseas empire, and the Republic led Europe in science, publishing and painting, with artists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rembrandt">Rembrandt</a>, Vermeer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frans-hals">Frans Hals</a>. It faded after the French invasion of 1672 and the costly wars that followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-golden-age.jpg" alt="Dutch Golden Age" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claes Janszoon Visscher II / Joannes van Doetecum I · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joannes_van_Deutecum_-_Leo_Belgicus_1650_-_published_by_Claes_Jansz_Visscher_Amsterdam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1568 the Seven Provinces that later signed the Union of Utrecht rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, beginning the Eighty Years' War. Antwerp, then arguably the most important port in the world, fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 17 August 1585 after a siege, and the division between the Northern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-netherlands">Southern Netherlands</a> was established. Under the terms of the surrender, Protestants unwilling to reconvert were given four years to leave. More Protestants moved north between 1585 and 1630 than Catholics moved south, and many settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, which became one of the most important ports and commercial centres in the world by 1630. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> formal recognition of its independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC), founded in 1602, was among the first multinational corporations, financed by shares that established the Amsterdam Stock Exchange. It received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> monopoly on Asian trade, which it kept for two centuries, and became the largest commercial enterprise of the 17th-century world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-amsterdam">Bank of Amsterdam</a> was established in 1609. The Republic's position between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> and the Baltic let its ships carry goods between the two, and the Dutch share of European shipping was well over half during most of the period. From 1640 the VOC held a monopoly on trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dejima">Dejima</a>, and until 1854 the Dutch were Japan's sole window to the Western world.</p><p>The prosperity came with violence. In 1621 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-pieterszoon-coen">Jan Pieterszoon Coen</a> had almost all the inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-islands">Banda Islands</a> massacred, and about 1.7 million people were enslaved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> slavers between the 17th and 19th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> was the state religion, though bitter controversy between strict Calvinists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remonstrants">Remonstrants</a> was settled in favour of the former at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-dort">Synod of Dort</a> in 1618–19.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Republic attracted scientists and thinkers from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>, founded in 1575, became a gathering place for intellectuals, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-descartes">René Descartes</a>, Jan Amos Comenius and Pierre Bayle all lived and published there. Wealthy merchants rather than nobles were the patrons of art. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> painting led in developing still life, landscape and genre painting, canals were dug and merchants built houses along them, and the book trade flourished, with the House of Elzevir producing reliable pocket editions of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> texts. Conflicts with neighbouring powers and declining economic influence ended the period, usually dated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rampjaar">Rampjaar</a> of 1672. Since 2019 the Amsterdam Museum has stopped using the phrase Golden Age, saying it hides the colonial past and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dutch Golden Age” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The painting of the <b>Dutch Golden Age</b>, by Rembrandt van Rijn, Johannes Vermeer, Frans Hals and many others, developed landscape, still life and scenes of everyday life as independent genres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
